The Burmese chronicles state that the Burmese invaded the Ayutthaya Kingdom in 1187 with an army of 40,000 men.[33] This number appears to be exaggerated by the Burmese, as the Burmese chronicles never mention any Chinese troops in the country at the time of the invasion.[34][35]

In 1277, the Mongols invaded the Ayutthaya Kingdom from the north. The Burmese chronicles do not specifically mention the invaders being Chinese, but instead mention them as "Taruk" (i.e. Mongol). The Mongols were led by the TatarBajazet, who quickly captured Ayutthaya. The Mongols sacked Ayutthaya, burned it to the ground, and looted the city for several days. They then marched south into the upper river regions of Thailand to the banks of the Chao Phraya River.
